\section{For extension authors}{

\code{frm_stop()}, \code{frm_warning()} and \code{frm_message()} are the functions
that frmtmb uses to raise its conditions. Use them in an extension
in place of \code{\link[=stop]{stop()}}, \code{\link[=warning]{warning()}} and \code{\link[=message]{message()}}. They take the
same arguments as the base functions and give the same message text
and the same call, and they add the classes in the table above. The
class of the extension is found from the namespace of the function
that calls them, so an extension does not supply it. A function of
base R between the two, such as \code{\link[=lapply]{lapply()}} when a helper is given as
its \code{FUN}, is skipped.

Given one condition object, the helpers signal that object, as the
base functions do, with the frmtmb classes added in front of its own
classes.

\code{class} adds classes in front of the others, for a condition that a
caller must be able to catch by itself. \code{package} names the package
whose subclass the condition gets, in place of the package of the
calling function. Use it where one package raises a refusal that
another package wrote. \code{package = "frmtmb"} gives no subclass.
\code{frm_family_package()} gives the package that built a family, the
value to use for a refusal about that family.

\code{frm_match_arg()} does what \code{\link[=match.arg]{match.arg()}} does, and a value that
matches no choice is a \code{frmtmb_error} that names the argument, the
value and the choices. The subclass is that of the function that
calls \code{frm_match_arg()}.

These differences from the base functions remain:
\itemize{
\item With \code{call. = TRUE}, an error that nothing catches in a
session that is not interactive can print one more line,
\verb{Calls:}, that includes the name of the helper. This line is R's
short traceback. The message and the call are the same as from
\code{\link[=stop]{stop()}}. frmtmb itself uses \code{call. = FALSE}.
\item \code{noBreaks.} has no effect.
\item \code{frm_message()} records the call to itself, where
\code{\link[=message]{message()}} records the call to \code{\link[=message]{message()}}. R does not print the
call of a message.
}
}

